
# The cask: a native window, the platform's WebView inside it, and a bridge that carries text both ways. This is
# the whole end-user surface, and it is the same eight tasks on every platform. An app names these and never a
# platform: the build rewrites the `{platform}` slot below to `swift` on macOS and iOS, `kotlin` on Android, `rust`
# on Windows and Linux, the way every stdlib module reaches its native half. Design: note/term/cask/readme.md.
#
#   open-window   title, width, height          -> window
#   load-bundle   window, path to a built page    the page shipped inside the app
#   load-url      window, url                    the dev loop, pointed at the Term dev server
#   eval          window, script                 run a script in the page, on the UI thread
#   on-message    window, handler                answer what the page posts through `window.term.post`. The handler
#                                                is asynchronous and returns the reply text
#   emit          window, name, text             push an event into the page
#   listen        name, handler                  receive an event, on the page side
#   on-ready      window, handler                the page has finished loading
#   on-close      window, handler                the window closed, by the person or by `close`. A cask's dispatcher
#                                                drops every handle that window's page held (native-dom-0030)
#   close         window                         close it as its close box does: every `on-close` handler runs
#   snapshot      window, path, done             write a PNG of the page as drawn, then call done
#   show          window                         put the window on screen, behind everything. A window opens off screen
#   activate      window                         bring the window to the front with focus
#   run                                          hand the process to the platform's event loop. Does not return
#   quit                                         leave it
#   exit          status                        leave it with a status
#   bundle-path                                  where the app's own files are
#   data-path     name                           where the app may write
#   home-path                                    the person's home directory, which a scope's `$home` names (the cask's
#                                                own: a page is not told it)
#   update-launch-path   resources, page, data    the page to load: the shipped one or the newest good update
#   update-check         resources, data, base, channel, done   fetch, verify and stage an update for the next launch
#   update-ready         data                    this launch's page rendered, so its update is good
#                                                (Swift, Kotlin and Rust; node stubs them)

bear @term/cask/native/{platform}/cask
